## HARDENING WAVE — 2026-07-13 (10/10 push: offline-completable hardening)
Scope: close every engineering-completable gap toward a 10/10 real-world readiness
that does not require a live instance, a Windows host, or an owner-gated npm
publish. Verified end-to-end: `npm run check` green (14 packages, 725 core + 943
mcp tests), all six governance gates pass, prod `npm audit` clean.

- [x] **HW-1. `completion` command (23rd CLI command)** — new
      `syncrona completion [bash|zsh]` prints a shell tab-completion script for the
      live command registry. Handler is completionCommand.ts (writes the script via
      `process.stdout.write`, kept byte-clean); registered in cliCommands.ts with
      bash/zsh choices + examples. README, CLAUDE.md and the CLAUDE-docs-drift gate
      updated (23 commands aligned with cliCommands.ts).
- [x] **HW-2. MCP output contract (`outputSchema` + `structuredContent`)** — the
      eight stable-shape tools (six session tools via `structuredJsonResponse`,
      plus `sync_status`/`sync_refresh` via `commandJsonResponse` on exit 0) declare
      an `outputSchema` and return conforming `structuredContent` on every success
      result (isError results exempt — the SDK v1.29.0 low-level Server does no
      runtime validation). Variable-shape CRUD tools intentionally excluded. The
      tool-reference generator renders an Output section per schema; contract +
      structuredContent assertions added to the mcp tests. Contract hash unchanged
      (ea90f026 over sorted tool names — schema additions never move it).
- [x] **HW-3. MCP stdio stdout purity** — for the protocol-channel commands
      (`mcp` inherits stdout as the JSON-RPC transport; `completion` pipes a script
      there), every bootstrap-phase notice is routed to stderr before loadConfigs()
      can log. bootstrap.ts `stdoutIsProtocolChannel(argv)` engages
      `logger.routeAllToStderr()` up front; mcpCommand.ts routes before setLogLevel;
      dotenv runs with `quiet: true`. 6 argv-parsing tests (bootstrap.test.ts) incl.
      `--log-level debug mcp` and the `--flag=value` form; the routing survives
      later setLogLevel rebuilds via a sticky flag.
- [x] **HW-4. MCP coverage gate raised to 90/80** — measured ~94.2% line / ~87.1%
      branch after the new handler/session tests; ratchet lifted from the prior
      floor. Core ratchet held at 92/79/89/92.

### Adversarial review of the wave (2026-07-13) — 3 findings, all fixed
A multi-dimension adversarial pass over the accumulated diff surfaced three real
defects (a Watcher requeue-stall candidate and two create-table-policy candidates
were verified and refuted). All three fixed with regression tests:
- [x] **REV-1. eslint-plugin linted the on-disk file, not the piped content** —
      packages/eslint-plugin/src/index.ts called `linter.lintFiles([filePath])`,
      re-reading raw disk bytes and ignoring the `content` it was handed. In a
      transform-then-lint plugin chain it validated the wrong (pre-transform)
      source; the sibling prettier/typescript plugins correctly operate on
      `content`. Fixed to `linter.lintText(content, { filePath })`; 2 tests that
      make disk and content disagree in both directions (previously masked because
      the tests wrote the same string they passed).
- [x] **REV-2. tool-reference generator degraded silently** — a non-inline schema
      value degraded the block to fallback parsing (dropping its Output section and
      param types) but neither generate nor `--check` returned non-zero, so the
      drift gate stayed green over an incomplete doc. Now fatal in both modes
      (consistent with extractToolBlocks' fatal spread handling); a CLI test covers
      generate-refuses and check-catches-a-committed-degraded-copy.
- [x] **REV-3. docs/VERSIONING.md said "22 commands"** — stale after the
      `completion` addition; the claims-drift gate only checks docs/index.html so
      CI missed it. Corrected to 23.

### Second adversarial pass (2026-07-13) — plugin-pipeline & offline-gate hardening
A follow-up multi-dimension audit of the build-plugin pipeline, the shared Jira
renderer, and the coverage-gate tooling surfaced seven more real defects (three
further candidates — a Watcher requeue stall and two create-table-policy paths —
were re-verified and refuted). All fixed with regression tests; `npm run check`
stays green (mcp tests 943 → 956; mcp coverage 94.13% line / 86.84% branch).
- [x] **REV-4. sass-plugin compiled the disk file, not the piped content** —
      `src/index.ts` re-read the entrypoint from disk instead of the `content` it
      was handed, so in a transform-then-compile chain it built the pre-transform
      bytes (the REV-1 class again). Fixed to
      `sass.compileString(content, { syntax, url: pathToFileURL(filePath) })` —
      extension-driven indented/scss syntax, and the file URL keeps relative
      `@use`/`@import` resolving against the entrypoint dir. 5 tests, incl. one
      where disk and content disagree and one resolving a relative `@use`.
- [x] **REV-5. typescript-plugin crashed on a tsconfig without compilerOptions** —
      a `tsconfig.json` with no `compilerOptions` key threw "Cannot set properties
      of undefined (setting 'rootDir')". Guarded by defaulting `compilerOptions` to
      `{}` before use. Regression test writes such a tsconfig beside the fixture.
- [x] **REV-6. typescript-plugin type-checked the disk bytes, not the content** —
      same REV-1 class: it type-checked the on-disk file while the pipeline had
      already produced corrected `content`. Fixed with an in-memory compiler-host
      overlay (getSourceFile/readFile/fileExists serve the passed content for the
      target file, delegating lib/node_modules to the base host). 2 tests make disk
      and content disagree in both directions; existing diagnostic behavior held.
- [x] **REV-7. babel-plugin mutated the caller's shared options** — it wrote
      `filename` back into the `options` object handed to every file in a build,
      leaking one file's identity into the next. Now builds a fresh options object;
      a test asserts `filename` never leaks into the caller's object.
- [x] **REV-8. babel-plugin treated empty output as a build failure** — the old
      `if (res && res.code)` guard failed a comment-only/empty source (whose
      transform is legitimately `""`). Now `typeof res.code === "string"` — empty
      output is a success. Test covers a comment-only source with `comments:false`.
- [x] **REV-9. jira adfToText threw RangeError on an out-of-range date** — a
      crafted ADF `date` node with a finite but out-of-range `attrs.timestamp` made
      `new Date(ms).toISOString()` throw, breaking the module's never-throws
      contract and aborting the whole issue fetch. Range-guarded (`|ms| <= 8.64e15`)
      to degrade to "" like every other bad input. Deterministic regression test.
- [x] **REV-10. check-coverage-gate.js silently ignored malformed flags** — a typo
      in a threshold flag (e.g. `--branch-threshhold 80`) left the gate at its
      default, most dangerously leaving the branch gate OFF (0) while the run still
      reported success. `parseArgs` now rejects unknown tokens and missing /
      non-numeric / out-of-0..100 values (and accepts `--flag=value`); a new
      `test/coverage-gate.test.js` (13 tests) covers both exported parsers.

### Third adversarial pass (2026-07-16) — full-repo sweep, 21 findings, all fixed
A third multi-dimension adversarial audit swept the whole monorepo (build plugins,
core pipeline, credential store, MCP runtime and its governance scripts) and
surfaced twenty-one real defects, every one adversarially re-verified (0 refuted).
All fixed with regression tests; `npm run check` stays green (core tests 740, mcp
tests 962). The recurring "operate on the disk file, not the piped `content`" class
(REV-1/REV-4/REV-6) reappeared in the last remaining build plugin, and two exit-code
and three governance-anchor bugs mirror earlier waves — evidence the sweep reached the
tail of each defect family.
- [x] **REV-11. webpack-plugin re-bundled the disk file, not the piped content** —
      `src/index.ts` ignored the passed-in `content` and re-read `context.filePath`
      from disk, discarding every prior transform in the chain (the REV-1/REV-4/REV-6
      class, now closed in the last build plugin). Fixed with an in-memory input
      overlay so webpack bundles the handed content. 2 tests make disk and content
      disagree in both directions.
- [x] **REV-12. babel-remove-modules leaked comment state across files** —
      `commentUsageTracker` lived in the plugin-factory closure keyed only by
      line+column (no filename), so one file's `@keepModule`/`@expandModule` usage
      bled into later files and tags were silently dropped. Reset per file in
      `pre()`; a test compiles two files whose tags share coordinates and asserts
      neither is dropped.
- [x] **REV-13. push --ci silently auto-resumed a stale checkpoint** — a leftover
      checkpoint made `push --ci` narrow (or entirely drop) the intended push and
      still exit 0. Now `--ci` validates the checkpoint and refuses a stale/mismatched
      one instead of silently resuming. Regression test drives the stale-checkpoint
      path.
- [x] **REV-14. resumed download blanked already-completed tables** — the skeleton
      force-write phase re-created completed tables as empty files, then the resume
      checkpoint skipped re-fetching them, so a resumed pull truncated the very
      tables it meant to preserve. Skeleton phase now leaves non-empty completed
      files intact (zero-byte still treated as missing). Tests cover forceWrite=false
      and ci mode.
- [x] **REV-15. webpack-plugin leaked compiler resources** — `compiler.run(...)` was
      never followed by `compiler.close()`, leaking webpack 5 caches, file handles
      and worker threads per processed file. Now closes the compiler in a `finally`.
      Test asserts close is invoked on both success and failure paths.
- [x] **REV-16. webpack-plugin swallowed config-load errors** — `loadWebpackConfig`
      caught every import error from `webpack.config.js` and returned `false`, so a
      config that exists but fails to load was silently ignored and the bundle built
      with default config. Now distinguishes "no config" from "config failed to load"
      and surfaces the latter. Regression test points at a throwing config.
- [x] **REV-17. non-atomic stale-lock reclaim let two pushes both acquire** —
      `acquireCollaborationLock` reclaimed a stale lock via a blind unlink+write, so
      two concurrent pushes could both win the collaboration lock. Reclaim is now
      atomic (ownership-checked). Test simulates the interleaving.
- [x] **REV-18. Table-API fallback marked 400/403/404 tables complete-but-empty** —
      a table returning 400/403/404 during the download fallback was dropped from the
      result yet marked complete and reported as "Download complete", leaving empty
      files. Now such tables are accounted as failures and set a non-zero exit code.
      Test drives each status.
- [x] **REV-19. findMissingFiles ignored flat layout** — it only checked the
      per-record folder layout, so `repair` reported every file missing (and
      re-downloaded the whole scope) in a flat-layout workspace. Now honors the
      configured layout. Test covers a flat-mode manifest.
- [x] **REV-20. failed push batch stalled with no retry** — after a failed push the
      in-flight batch was requeued into `pushQueue` but no drain was rescheduled, so
      the changes stalled forever. Now schedules a bounded auto-retry drain. Test
      asserts a requeued batch is drained again.
- [x] **REV-21. wizard source-dir produced an invalid config file** —
      `getDefaultConfigFile()` interpolated the free-text source directory into a
      double-quoted JS string with no escaping, so a backslash/quote in the input
      generated a syntactically invalid `sync.config.js` that bricked every later
      command. Now `JSON.stringify`-quoted. Test feeds a path with a quote/backslash.
- [x] **REV-22. docsCommand exited 0 on failure** — it logged failures via
      `logger.error` but never set `process.exitCode`, so `syncrona docs` reported
      success when generation failed or no manifest existed. Now sets a non-zero exit
      code. Regression test asserts the exit code on both failure arms.
- [x] **REV-23. instanceToFilename was lossy and non-reversible** — its `replace()`
      let two distinct instance names collide onto one credential file (silently
      overwriting) and could not be round-tripped by `filenameToInstance` — the class
      the twin `jiraProfileToFilename` was already rewritten with `encodeURIComponent`
      to avoid. Now reversible/collision-free. Test round-trips names that previously
      collided.
- [x] **REV-24. scope name unescaped in Table-API encoded query** — the config scope
      name was interpolated into encoded queries without `escapeQueryValue` (unlike
      `snClient.getScopeId`), allowing `^`-injection of extra conditions. Now escaped.
      Test asserts an injected `^` is neutralized.
- [x] **REV-25. buildRecordName didn't guard `.`/`..`** — it sanitized only `/` and
      `\\`, so a record named `.` or `..` wrote its field files outside the record
      directory (then `repair --apply --prune` deleted them). Now guards dot names.
      Test covers `.` and `..`.
- [x] **REV-26. config vm sandbox got the real stdout console** — the vm evaluating
      `sync.config.js` was handed the real stdout-writing `console`; bootstrap's
      stdout-purity routing only redirected the winston logger, so config-side
      `console.log` escaped onto the MCP/completion protocol channel. Sandbox console
      now routes to stderr. Test asserts nothing reaches stdout.
- [x] **REV-27. healthServer dropped its error listener after listen()** — the only
      `'error'` handler was removed once `listen()` succeeded, so any later
      server-level error had no listener and Node re-threw it, crashing the whole MCP
      process. A persistent logging handler now stays attached. Test emits a
      post-listen `'error'` and asserts no crash.
- [x] **REV-28. claims-drift auth gate matched a page-wide substring** — the
      auth-method documentation gate used a bare page-wide substring match, so the
      word "basic" (e.g. in "basically") satisfied the check even with Basic-auth
      docs removed. Now scoped to the `id="security"` section with word-boundary
      matching. Tests cover the "basically" false-positive and a missing section.
- [x] **REV-29. release-checklist section check was substring-loose** — required
      governance sections were checked with `includes(section)`, so a required
      `## Section` demoted to `### Section` (or buried in prose/a code fence) still
      passed. Now anchored to a line-start heading of the exact level. Test demotes
      every required section and asserts all are flagged.
- [x] **REV-30. claude-docs-drift section check was substring-loose** — same
      looseness as REV-29 for required CLAUDE.md sections. Now anchored via
      `hasHeadingLine`. Test demotes a required section to a deeper level and asserts
      it is flagged.
- [x] **REV-31. doctorCommand exited 0 on a broken workspace** — it computed
      `ok=false` when checks failed but never set `process.exitCode`, so
      `syncrona doctor` exited 0 while its sibling `checkEnvCommand` exited 1 in the
      same situation. Now sets a non-zero exit code on failure. Regression test
      asserts it.

### Fourth adversarial pass (2026-07-16) — full-repo sweep, 13 findings, all fixed
A fourth multi-agent adversarial audit swept the whole monorepo (core download/push/
config/diagnostics, MCP handlers/runtime/governance, shared libs, and every build
plugin). It surfaced thirteen real defects, each double-verified by two diverse
skeptics (0 refuted). All fixed with regression tests proven to fail on the old code
and pass on the fix; `npm run check` stays green (core tests 747, mcp 967,
credential-store 60, typescript-plugin 8, babel-remove-modules 10). The findings
cluster at the tail of prior families — resume/round-trip correctness (REV-13/21/23),
config-vm realm handling (REV-26), gate soundness (REV-2/28/29/30) and exit-code gaps
(REV-22/31) — plus a compiler-API contract bug the earlier typescript-plugin pass
(REV-5) did not reach.
- [x] **REV-32. push checkpoint resumed a grown diff, dropping new records** —
      the checkpoint-match test only verified previously-*failed* keys were still in
      the current diff (a subset check in one direction), so when the diff GREW between
      runs the resume filter narrowed `fileList` down to the old failed keys and the
      newly-changed records were silently never pushed. Now the checkpoint is reused
      only when the current work is also a subset of what was already *attempted*
      (`fileList.every(rec => attemptedSet.has(recToCheckpointKey(rec)))`); otherwise it
      is discarded and the full diff is pushed. Test drives a grown diff and asserts the
      new record is not dropped.
- [x] **REV-33. push checkpoint ignored a switched target instance** — `PushCheckpoint`
      stored no instance, so resuming after `syncrona use <other>` skipped records that
      had succeeded on the original instance but were never sent to the new one. The
      checkpoint now records the resolved instance and is discarded on mismatch. Test
      resumes against a different instance and asserts a full re-push.
- [x] **REV-34. PluginManager rejected cross-realm RegExp match rules** — `determinePlugins`
      gated plugin rules with `reg instanceof RegExp`, but `sync.config.js` is evaluated
      through `vm.runInNewContext`, so a regex literal in the config is created with the
      vm realm's `RegExp` intrinsic and fails `instanceof` cross-realm — silently
      disabling *every* regex-matched build transform. Now uses realm-agnostic
      `util.types.isRegExp`. Test feeds a vm-produced regex and asserts the rule matches.
- [x] **REV-35. .env writer corrupted secrets with backslashes or quotes** —
      `formatEnvValue` wrapped non-simple values in double quotes and backslash-escaped
      `\` and `"`, but dotenv v17 does not un-escape backslashes in double-quoted values
      (it only expands `\n`/`\r`), so `Win\2026` was written `"Win\\2026"` and read back
      corrupted; `a"b` and any backslash value likewise. Now wraps in single quotes with
      no escaping (dotenv returns single-quoted values verbatim, matching first-to-last
      quote), which round-trips every case including embedded `'`. The docstring's false
      round-trip-safety claim was corrected. Test now round-trips through the real
      `dotenv.parse` for seven adversarial values instead of only asserting the produced
      string.
- [x] **REV-36. isValidScope dropped every file when the workspace is the repo root** —
      when the scope IS the git root, `path.relative(root, scope)` is `""`, so
      `startsWith("" + "/")` was false for every path and `=== ""` never matched — so
      `push --diff` / `build --diff` silently operated on zero files. Now an empty
      relative path means "the scope contains the whole repo" and returns true. Test
      asserts every changed file stays in scope at the repo root.
- [x] **REV-37. manifest record query omitted the configured display/differentiator
      fields** — `getRecordsForTable` never SELECTed the configured `displayField` or
      `differentiatorField`, so the manifest it built diverged from what the download
      path fetched; `findMissingFiles` then false-missed and `repair --prune` deleted
      freshly-downloaded files. A shared `buildRecordFieldList()` now feeds both the
      manifest query and the bulk-download path. Test asserts both option fields appear
      in the record query.
- [x] **REV-38. mcp command exited 0 on the no-credentials failure** — the
      'no credentials' branch logged an error and returned without setting
      `process.exitCode`, so `syncrona mcp` reported success on a real failure (the
      REV-22/31 exit-code family). Now sets a non-zero exit code before returning. Test
      asserts `exitCode === 1` on the no-login path.
- [x] **REV-39. preflight-check emitted a false all-clear on empty overrides** —
      `sync_preflight_check` forwarded empty-string `expected*` overrides to
      `buildPreflightReport`, clobbering the guardrail/config expectations so the
      comparison trivially matched and returned an unconditional `allOk:true`. Now only
      actually-supplied (non-empty) override fields are forwarded via conditional spread.
      Tests cover both the empty-override false-clear and that a real override is still
      honored.
- [x] **REV-40. docs-drift gate missed a stale entry for a removed last-of-family
      tool** — the gate derived the allowed doc tool-name set only from the *surviving*
      schema's family prefixes, so removing the last member of a family erased its prefix
      and a now-stale doc entry for that tool was no longer flagged. Doc extraction now
      matches a generic declaration-site tool-name shape instead of surviving-family
      prefixes, so a removed-last-of-family tool left in the docs is detected as
      `extraInDocs`. Tests cover the removed-last-of-family case end to end.
- [x] **REV-41. recent-changes echoed a bound it never applied** — a non-empty but
      unparseable `since` produced an empty ServiceNow datetime (the time-bound clause
      was dropped) while the payload still echoed the raw `since`, so callers received
      all-time results reported as bounded. Now an unparseable `since` falls back to
      `defaultSinceIso()` and the payload echoes the bound actually applied. Test asserts
      the query carries a real `sys_created_on>=` and never echoes the raw input.
- [x] **REV-42. one undecodable credential filename hid all instances** —
      `listInstances` decoded every `.enc` filename inside a single try/catch, so one
      malformed name aborted the whole loop and returned `[]`, hiding all stored
      instances and turning `removeAllCredentials` into a silent no-op. Moved the
      try/catch inside the per-file loop (mirroring the already-correct
      `listJiraProfiles`), so a bad name is skipped and the rest survive. Tests seed a
      malformed name alongside valid ones and assert the valid instances still list and
      remove.
- [x] **REV-43. typescript-plugin fed raw JSON compilerOptions to createProgram** —
      it passed tsconfig `compilerOptions` (string enums like `target:"ES2017"`,
      `lib:["ES2017","DOM"]`) straight into `ts.createProgram`, which TS 5.5+ rejects
      ("tsconfig JSON must be parsed ... before passing to createProgram"), breaking the
      plugin on any real tsconfig. Now converts via `ts.convertCompilerOptionsFromJson`
      (surfacing `.errors`), which also removed the buggy manual lib-name casing hack
      (`lib.ES2017.d.ts` → correct `lib.es2017.d.ts`). Test runs a realistic string-enum
      tsconfig and asserts a non-throwing transpile.
- [x] **REV-44. babel-remove-modules never renamed aliased @expandModule imports** —
      for `@expandModule` specifiers it collected `imported.name` (the original export)
      instead of `local.name` (the binding actually in scope), so `path.scope.rename`
      never matched an aliased import (`import { foo as bar }`) and left a dangling
      `bar`. Now renames the local binding to `${moduleName}.${importedName}`, handles
      default specifiers, and no longer ignores `import * as ns`. Test transforms an
      aliased import and asserts `bar()` becomes `mod.foo()` with no dangling `bar`.

Deliberately left ungated: internal snapshot docs (PRODUCT_STATE.md, VERSIONING.md)
carry point-in-time numbers (readiness/coverage/test counts) by design. The
user-facing claims (README, COMPARISON.md, index.html) are machine-gated by
claims-drift; PRODUCT_STATE.md's stale "22 commands" was corrected to 23 in place
rather than adding a fragile gate over a dated snapshot.

### Fifth and sixth adversarial passes (2026-07-17 … 2026-07-21) — REV-82 … REV-129
Those two waves are recorded in `WORKLOG.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` rather than here;
their tags survive in the code and in the regression tests that pin them.

### Seventh adversarial pass (2026-07-25) — full-repo sweep, 59 findings, all fixed
A seventh multi-agent adversarial audit swept all 14 packages plus `scripts/`, the
CI workflow and the user-facing docs. Triage: 108 raw findings → 103 unique → 18
low-severity set aside → 85 sent to verification → **59 confirmed** (20 high, 39
medium), 26 refuted. Every finding was judged by a three-lens panel (`reproduce` /
`already-handled` / `consequence`), each lens instructed to default to *refuted*
under uncertainty, and survived only on a 2-of-3 majority — so "refuted" here means
*not proven*, not *proven safe* (the six refuted **high** findings are carried in
ROADMAP.md for a second look). The 59 confirmed findings were closed under 37 REV
tags: some tags cover several sites of one defect class, and three tags (REV-140,
REV-141, REV-191) were reused for unrelated defects and are split into lettered
entries below, plus REV-193, which the regression tests for one of the fixes turned
up. Every fix ships a regression test proven to fail on the old code.
- [x] **REV-130. an explicit `--profile` fell back to ambient Jira env credentials** —
      both `resolveJiraConfig` and its sync twin computed
      `configFromStored(await loadJiraCredentials(profile)) ?? configFromEnv(env)`.
      `loadJiraCredentials` returns `null` for three indistinguishable situations (no
      such profile, deleted profile, undecryptable record), so `syncrona jira --profile
      prod` — or a typo — authenticated against whatever tenant `JIRA_BASE_URL`/`_EMAIL`/
      `_TOKEN` happened to point at and reported success. It also made the MCP runtime's
      `undecryptable` health branch unreachable. Now an explicit profile returns
      `configFromStored(...)` directly so `null` propagates and the real cause is
      reported; the ambient-env fallback survives only on the default (no-profile) path.
- [x] **REV-131. an ADF `expand` block dropped its title** — `expand`/`nestedExpand`
      keep their heading in `attrs.title` and had no case in `renderBlock`, so they fell
      through to the generic block path that renders only children. Every rendered issue
      silently lost the label above the prose ("Acceptance criteria", "Rollback plan").
      New `renderExpand` emits `title\nbody`.
- [x] **REV-132. the boundary self-test counted non-`error` rules as enforced** —
      `findUnfiredRules` treated any entry in `summary.violations` as proof a rule fires,
      but dependency-cruiser exits on `summary.error`: a boundary rule demoted to `warn`
      produced violations, exited 0, and looked fully enforced while enforcing nothing.
      The fixture was also cruised with a hand-written option object instead of the real
      `.dependency-cruiser.cjs`. Now `loadCruiseOptions()` reads the real config, the
      fired-rule filter is `severity === 'error'`, and `findNonErrorRules` surfaces every
      rule that cannot fail the build.
- [x] **REV-133. the CI `check` job never ran `verify:pack`** — the workflow
      hand-replicates the root `check` chain step by step for per-step annotations, and
      that duplication drifted: `verify:pack`, the only gate that inspects tarball
      *contents* (secret files, stray sources, missing `bin`/`dist`, undeclared runtime
      deps), was never added. A `files:`/`bin:` regression could reach the release branch
      with CI green. The step was added, and a test now re-derives the chain from the root
      `package.json` and asserts every link is executed by the workflow.
- [x] **REV-134. the pack bin smoke symlinked the whole hoisted `node_modules`** — it
      linked every entry of the workspace and repo-root `node_modules` into the staged
      tarball, so an import of a merely hoisted package (a devDependency, or a transitive
      dep of an unrelated package) resolved in the smoke and then crashed with
      `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` for the first real user — the exact failure the gate exists to
      catch. Only the transitive closure of the **declared** runtime dependencies is now
      staged.
- [x] **REV-135. SECURITY.md claimed every mutating tool requires
      `confirmDestructive=true`** — `sync_set_scope`, `sync_set_update_set` and
      `sync_prepare_session` are in `MUTATING_TOOLS`, change the integration user's
      session on the instance, and have no confirmation flag at all. A reader hardening an
      MCP deployment was told a control existed that does not. The document now names
      which tools are gated and which are not; a test re-derives the list from
      `MCP_TOOLS`/`isMutatingTool` so it cannot drift again.
- [x] **REV-136. SECURITY.md promised redaction of the diagnostic log** — it claimed
      "best-effort redaction of known credential fields", but the file transport applies
      uncolorize + timestamp + printf and nothing else: there is no redactor. Corrected to
      state plainly that no redactor runs over that file, and pinned by a test.
- [x] **REV-137. README documented a `pull` command and a `flat` conversion that do not
      exist** — `pull` was never a command, and switching `flat` does not re-lay an
      existing workspace on the next `refresh` (nothing converts an existing tree). Both
      claims corrected and re-derived from the code by the same test file.
- [x] **REV-138. `.changeset/config.json` left the flagship package out of the release
      family** — the fixed group was pinned with the single pattern `@syncrona/*`, which
      does not match the CLI: it publishes **unscoped** as `syncrona`. Changesets expands
      patterns with micromatch and only warns about a pattern matching *nothing*, so the
      gap was silent: `changeset version` would have moved every scoped package in
      lockstep and left `syncrona` behind, contradicting docs/VERSIONING.md and shipping a
      CLI whose pinned `@syncrona/*` ranges match nothing published.
- [x] **REV-139. the depcruise fixture had no `node_modules`, so resolved-path rules
      could never fire** — sibling of REV-132 in the same script. The synthetic fixture
      omitted `node_modules`, so every `@syncrona/*` import stayed an unresolved specifier
      and any rule keyed on a dependency's *resolved* path could not match — the self-test
      could not tell "this rule is dead" from "this fixture cannot reach it".
      `writeViolatingFixtures` now materialises real package names, `dist` entry points, a
      tsconfig and workspace symlinks, so the fixture resolves the way the workspace does.
- [x] **REV-140a. plugins were imported by directory, which Node ESM refuses** — plugins
      were imported by bare package name, and Node's ESM loader cannot import a directory
      (`ERR_UNSUPPORTED_DIR_IMPORT`). The generic handler surfaced that as the "plugin not
      installed" hint, so a correctly installed plugin reported itself missing and was
      silently skipped; a second bug unwrapped the namespace as `loaded?.default ?? loaded`
      unconditionally, picking the wrong object for a plugin exporting `run` at top level.
      `resolvePluginSpecifier` now resolves the package to its entry **file** through
      `createRequire(<rootDir>/package.json)` + `pathToFileURL`, and `runPlugins` prefers
      the namespace when `typeof loaded?.run === "function"`.
- [x] **REV-140b. `repair --apply --prune` deleted live files whose on-disk name is
      encoded differently** — prune compared the manifest key to the filename byte-for-byte.
      macOS stores names in NFD while the manifest key is NFC, and Windows strips trailing
      dots and spaces at creation, so a tracked, live file looked like an orphan and was
      **deleted**. Unlink failures were swallowed (`.catch(() => undefined)`), so a
      partially failed prune still exited 0, and nothing stopped prune when the source path
      resolved to the repository root. Names are now canonicalised
      (`normalize("NFC").replace(/[.\s]+$/u, "")`), any file claimed under *any* encoding is
      kept, prune refuses when source path === repo root, and failed unlinks set exit 1.
- [x] **REV-140c. a table whose fields were withheld was checkpointed as a success** —
      the completeness guard only detected a table that returned *nothing*. A column-level
      read ACL still returns the rows, just without the restricted field; the pipeline
      wrote the resume checkpoint and moved on, so the missing field looked like an
      intentionally empty value and a later resume never re-tried the table. New
      `collectUnfetchedFields(requested, fetched)` diffs asked-for against returned fields
      and pushes a field-level gap to `failedTables` without checkpointing.
- [x] **REV-140d. a read-only `git` verb's own options were never inspected** — the
      confirmation gate allowlists read-only `git` subcommands and **returned** as soon as
      it recognised one, so every token after the subcommand went unexamined. Several
      read-only verbs accept options that name a program to run or a file to overwrite, so
      an allowlisted verb was a general execution primitive — verified against real git
      2.50.1: `git grep -nOtouch\ /tmp/PWN hello` executes `touch` with no confirmation,
      and `git help -w`/`-m`/`-i` hand the topic to an external viewer. The walk now
      continues past the verb and checks each token with `isSafeGitSubcommandOption` against
      a default-deny allowlist; anything unrecognised requires confirmation.
- [x] **REV-140e. (test soundness) the sandbox mock hid the arguments it asserted** — the
      `runCommand` mock bound only `(command, cmdArgs, timeoutMs)`, so the scrubbed base env
      the sandbox test exists to verify was never captured and the assertion could not
      observe the value it claimed to check. The mock now binds all six parameters and the
      env is asserted at the call site. Extends the pre-existing SEC-1/REV-116 work rather
      than covering a new defect.
- [x] **REV-141a. the core coverage floors ignored every file in a `src/` subdirectory** —
      the per-file threshold groups were the non-recursive globs `'./src/*.ts'` and
      `'./src/!(index).ts'`. Jest puts a file matching no per-file group into the **global**
      bucket, where it is averaged away, so every source file under a `src/` subdirectory
      (handlers, helpers, plugins) had no per-file floor and could drop to near-zero without
      failing the ratchet. Both globs are now recursive.
- [x] **REV-141b. an allowlisted binary *name* at a caller-chosen *path* bypassed the
      gate** — the safe-command allowlist matched `normalizeBinaryName(command)`, i.e. the
      basename only, so `./git status`, `/tmp/evil/git status` or `..\git status` inherited
      the allowlist entry for `git` and ran without destructive confirmation — although the
      program executed is whatever the caller planted there. `requiresConfirmation` now
      requires confirmation for any command containing `/` or `\` before the allowlist is
      consulted.
- [x] **REV-142. wholesale erasure of the audit log read as healthy or merely "missing"** —
      the tamper-evident chain was anchored only in the file itself, leaving three erasure
      paths: `seq` was derived from the file alone (delete the log and the chain restarts at
      1); `validateAuditChain` returned early when the log was `!started`, **before** reading
      the out-of-band high-water marker, so a log replaced with unchained "legacy"-shaped
      JSON validated clean; and the missing-file branch reported `status: "ok"` without ever
      consulting the marker. Now the marker is read first, `seq` is
      `max(prev.seq, hw.seq) + 1`, a missing log below the high-water is `tampered`, and a
      chain starting below the marker reports "chain erased below high-water seq" — while
      rotation and quarantine stay legitimate fresh starts.
- [x] **REV-143. the audit high-water marker lived in world-writable `/tmp`** — the
      out-of-band marker REV-142 depends on was written under `os.tmpdir()`, so on Linux any
      local user could pre-plant or rewrite it and neutralise the whole scheme; and
      `writeFileSync` **follows symlinks**, so a planted link redirected the write to an
      arbitrary file owned by the running user, with an empty error handler hiding the
      failure. The marker now lives in `SYNCRONA_AUDIT_STATE_DIR` / `$XDG_STATE_HOME` /
      `~/.syncrona/audit-integrity` created `mode: 0o700`, non-regular targets are refused,
      and a failed write warns.
- [x] **REV-144. the integrity check itself followed a symlinked audit path** — the write
      path already refused a symlinked audit directory or file, but
      `checkAuditLogIntegrity` — which reads, rewrites and renames the audit file — had no
      such guard, so a planted symlink turned a routine check into an arbitrary-file
      rewrite/rename with the server's privileges. It now returns
      `status: "error"`, `reason: "audit path is a symlink"` without touching the target.
- [x] **REV-145. the secret scanner failed *open* above its 8 KB budget** —
      `looksLikeSecretValue` returned `false` ("not a secret") for any value over
      `SECRET_SCAN_BUDGET`, and that check gates redaction, so any credential embedded in a
      value larger than 8 KB (a PEM bundle, a long request body, a base64 blob) was written
      to the audit log in the clear: the cheap way to defeat redaction was to make the value
      big. The budget now fails **closed** — an unscannable value is redacted.
- [x] **REV-146. concurrent audit writers could emit the same `seq`** — appending to the
      chain is a read-modify-write (read tail → derive `seq`/`prevHash` → append → update
      high-water) and nothing serialised it, so two writers could both emit the same `seq`
      with the same `prevHash`. That state is unrecoverable: the log then permanently
      validates as `tampered` ("sequence gap"), destroying the forensic value of every later
      record; the rotation branch raced the same way. An `O_EXCL` lockfile
      (`openSync(lockPath, "wx")`, which also never follows a symlink) now serialises the
      critical section across processes, with a 10 s stale reclaim. *Disclosed residual:*
      after `AUDIT_LOCK_MAX_WAIT_MS = 500` the append deliberately proceeds **unlocked** so
      that losing the lock never loses a record — under sustained contention beyond 500 ms
      the original race is still reachable.
- [x] **REV-147. ordinary prose was redacted as an inline `Authorization` header** — the
      detector was "the scheme keyword plus anything after it", so a sentence like *"Basic
      authentication failed for user jdoe"* matched and the **whole** entry text was
      replaced by a placeholder — the audit trail lost the diagnostic information an
      operator reads it for, on the most common class of message. Detection is now split
      into a real header-value pattern plus a credential-shaped token after the scheme
      keyword.
- [x] **REV-148. a malformed guardrail node parsed as "no policy", the permissive
      default** — every nested guardrail node went through `asRecord()`, which coerces a
      string, number or array to `{}`. `policy: "strict"`, `policy.tools: [...]` or
      `policy.environments: 'prod'` therefore parsed as **no policy configured**, with the
      fail-closed `invalid` marker left unset: `evaluateToolPolicy()` allowed every tool
      including `sync_push`, and `getEffectiveAllowFullNodeAccess()` fell back to the
      top-level flag. The sibling checks already failed closed, so this was an inconsistency
      as well as a hole. `isMalformedConfigNode` now marks `policy`, `policy.tools` and
      `policy.environments` invalid with a specific reason; `undefined`/`null` keep their
      "absent" meaning.
- [x] **REV-149. `run_workspace_command` and `run_node_code` ignored `dryRun`** — both
      accepted the flag and ignored it entirely. Because the guardrail layer *does* honour
      it, `dryRun: true` was strictly worse than omitting it: the tool ran for real **and**
      the preflight/policy path treated the call as a simulation, disabling a safety gate on
      a live execution. Both handlers now return `makeDryRunAuditResponse(...)` before doing
      any work — `run_node_code` after the `allowFullNodeAccess` gate, so a dry run cannot
      probe whether full node access is enabled.
- [x] **REV-150. `sync_unified_change_workflow` applied for real under `dryRun`** — the
      workflow module was the one mutating module never handed `ctx.dryRun`, and `index.ts`
      **skips the preflight gate** for dry runs, so `dryRun: true` performed the real apply
      with a safety gate disabled — for a remote apply, dispatching the background script.
      `dryRun`/`makeDryRunAuditResponse` were added to `WorkflowContext` and plumbed through
      `TOOL_HANDLER_MODULES` and `executeMcpToolIntegration`, and the flag is declared in the
      zod and JSON schemas like every other mutating tool.
- [x] **REV-151. the audit record trusted the *requested* dry run, not the effective one** —
      both audit writers and the semantic-index invalidation read `args.dryRun === true`, so
      for a tool that ignores the flag (the REV-149/REV-150 class) the run was real while the
      record claimed a simulation, and the index was left un-invalidated after a workspace
      change. `DRY_RUN_AWARE_TOOLS` now enumerates the 14 tools whose handler actually
      branches on `dryRun`, and both paths use `isEffectiveDryRun(toolName, args)`.
- [x] **REV-152. `search_scripts` reported failed table queries as a clean zero-match
      result** — the handler collected per-table `errors` but always returned success. A
      failed table query searched **nothing**, so "0 matches" is *unknown*, not *negative*,
      yet the response was indistinguishable from a genuinely empty search — a caller could
      conclude "this pattern exists nowhere" from a run where every query failed on ACLs.
      The payload now carries `searchComplete` and the response is flagged as an error when
      it is false, even if matches were found.
- [x] **REV-153. the OAuth token poster pinned one undici dispatcher and ignored TLS
      material in its cache key** — `getDispatcher(config)` was called once at token-manager
      creation and the Agent was captured by the poster closure forever, so an in-place
      client-certificate rotation was never picked up and, once the LRU evicted and
      **closed** that Agent, the poster kept using a closed one. Separately,
      `tokenManagerKey` carried no TLS material, so two configs differing only in
      `tlsCustom`/`caBundlePath`/`clientCertPath`/`clientKeyPath`/`rejectUnauthorized` shared
      one token manager — one client certificate and one token. The dispatcher is now
      resolved per POST and the key includes all five TLS fields.
- [x] **REV-154. an invalidation raised while the index was building was swallowed** — the
      async build cleared the dirty flag when it finished, so a workspace change landing
      *during* the walk set the flag and the completing build cleared it, publishing an index
      that never saw the change and marking it clean — served indefinitely, because nothing
      would set the flag again. `builtAt` also recorded when the walk finished, making the
      index look newer than the tree state it captured. `INVALIDATION_EPOCH` is now bumped by
      every invalidation and the flag is cleared only when the build's epoch still matches;
      `builtAt` records the walk start.
- [x] **REV-155. deleting source files never marked the semantic index stale** — staleness
      was decided by the newest mtime in the source tree, and deletion raises no surviving
      file's mtime, so removing sources out of band left the index reported fresh — in the
      extreme, wiping every source pinned the pre-deletion index and the tools kept answering
      from files that no longer exist. The walk now also counts files and `isSampleStale`
      compares both signals.
- [x] **REV-156. the async staleness poll ran the synchronous filesystem walk** —
      `isSemanticIndexStaleAsync`, reached on the **cache-hit** path (the common case),
      called the synchronous `sampleSourceTree`, so every cached tool call performed a full
      synchronous workspace walk on the event loop and a burst of calls repeated it once per
      call. An `fs/promises` twin now serves the async path and the poll result is memoized
      for 500 ms.
- [x] **REV-157. a stored ServiceNow password was mangled by env-value cleaning** —
      `cleanEnvValue` trims whitespace and strips one leading/trailing quote pair, which is
      right for a value scraped from `.env` and wrong for a secret handed over by the
      credential store. Store-origin passwords were run through it (and the merge loop re-ran
      it per provider), so a password with a trailing space or a quote character was silently
      altered before being sent, and the only symptom was an unexplainable authentication
      failure. Store-origin secrets are now used verbatim, with presence semantics preserved
      so a whitespace-only password still counts as absent.
- [x] **REV-158. four attacker-controlled Jira fields reached the model unfenced** — the
      indirect-prompt-injection fence covered only `summary`, `description` and comment
      bodies, leaving four channels of identical provenance open: `comments[].author` (a
      display name the commenter sets), `parent.summary`, `subtasks[].summary` and
      `links[].issue.summary` — prose from **other** issues, pulled in automatically, so an
      attacker who cannot touch the target issue only has to link one of their own. The same
      server exposes code-execution tools, and a partial fence is worse than none because it
      advertises which fields are safe to trust. All four now go through
      `wrapUntrustedData(..., "jira")`; structural fields stay untouched. *Disclosed gap:*
      the issue-level `assignee`/`reporter` display names are still passed through verbatim,
      documented in the code as an honest limit and a deliberate follow-up.
- [x] **REV-160. `syncrona build` exited 0 after per-record build failures** —
      `AppUtils.buildFiles` never throws: `pushPipeline` folds per-record failures into
      `{ success: false }` results and `logBuildResults` only prints them, so the surrounding
      `catch` could not fire and a build with failed records still exited 0 — a CI step like
      `syncrona build && syncrona deploy` went on to deploy stale artifacts. Now sets exit 1
      when any result failed, mirroring `deployCommand`; `--dry-run` returns before the check.
- [x] **REV-161. `init` configured the MCP server after a cancelled or failed wizard** —
      `startWizard()` returned identically whether it completed, failed or was cancelled with
      Ctrl-C, and `initCommand` ignored the outcome, so `syncrona init` wrote an MCP
      configuration pointing at a project with no config, no manifest and no credentials.
      `startWizard` is now `Promise<boolean>` (and sets exit 130 on a prompt abort) and
      `initCommand` skips auto-configuration unless it returns true.
- [x] **REV-170. data-path requests had no timeout and hung forever** — the axios client
      used for every ServiceNow data request was created without a `timeout`, and axios waits
      indefinitely by default, so an instance behind a blackholing firewall, a dropped VPN or
      an accepted-but-unanswered connection left `download`/`refresh`/`push` hanging with no
      output and no way to tell it from slow progress. `resolveRequestTimeout()` now bounds
      every request at 120 s, overridable with `SN_REQUEST_TIMEOUT` (junk or negative falls
      back to the default; `0` deliberately restores unbounded behaviour).
- [x] **REV-171. the OAuth token client had no timeout** — the same defect on a different
      client and worse in effect: every authenticated request waits on token acquisition, so
      one hung token endpoint stalled the entire CLI rather than a single call. `tokenHttp`
      now uses the same resolved timeout.
- [x] **REV-172. an incompletely configured auth method silently downgraded to Basic** —
      every branch of `buildClientAuth` requires both a selected method *and* its credential
      material (`api-key` + `apiKey`; the OAuth grants + `clientId`/`clientSecret`;
      `oauth-jwt-bearer` also + `jwtKey`); with the method selected but the material
      incomplete, all branches fell through to `return {}`, which the snClient factory reads
      as "no OAuth, no API key" — i.e. plain **Basic** with whatever user/password is around.
      A half-configured api-key or JWT setup therefore sent the user's password under a
      weaker scheme, and the only symptom was a confusing 401 — or success with the wrong
      identity. `missingAuthMaterial` now names every missing value and `buildClientAuth`
      throws, pointing at `SN_AUTH_METHOD` / `syncrona login --auth-method`.
- [x] **REV-190. a bare `user:pass@host` pattern matched package specs** — same
      over-redaction class as REV-147 in the sibling pattern: the detector accepted any token
      after the `@`, so package specifiers and record references matched and were redacted
      **whole**, losing the identifiers an operator needs to reconstruct what a tool call did.
      The host part must now be an FQDN, `localhost` or an IPv4 literal.
- [x] **REV-191a. the tolerated "legacy prefix" of the audit chain was unbounded** — for
      backward compatibility the chain walk skips unchained lines preceding the first chained
      record, and that tolerance was unbounded and unpinned: any number of unchained records
      could be prepended to an intact chain and would simply be skipped, and a legacy line
      could be deleted undetected — so records could be forged into the log, or pre-chain
      history removed, while the chain validated clean. The legacy-prefix **count** is now
      part of the high-water marker and a change is reported as tampering.
- [x] **REV-191b. the git option scan bailed out at the first letter of a short-flag
      cluster** — direct follow-up to REV-140d: the new post-subcommand scan handled combined
      short flags (`-abc`) but stopped at the first letter that is value-taking for *some*
      verb, treating the rest of the cluster as that flag's value, so a dangerous flag hidden
      behind such a letter was never inspected — reopening exactly the hole REV-140d closed.
      Every letter of the cluster is now checked; verified against real git 2.50.1.
- [x] **REV-192. a malformed guardrail *entry* still parsed as "no policy"** — REV-148
      stopped the fail-open `asRecord()` coercion at the container level, but the entry values
      one level deeper still went through it: `policy.tools: { sync_push: 'deny' }`,
      `{ sync_push: ['deny'] }` or `policy.environments: { prod: 'strict' }` coerced to `{}`
      and parsed as no policy at all — the most dangerous shape being a config that *intends*
      to deny a tool and denies nothing. Each entry is now checked with the same
      `isMalformedConfigNode` predicate and sets a specific reason; `null` still means
      "absent".
- [x] **REV-193. a partially refused table read was still committed as authoritative** —
      found while writing the regression tests for REV-133's `onSkip` contract. The three
      deeper skip reports (text-field fallback, `sys_metadata` lookup, per-chunk `sys_idIN`
      fallback) all reached the builder, but the caller only acted on the skip when the
      table came back with **zero** records. A refused `sys_idIN` chunk leaves the other
      chunks intact, so the table returns a partial record set — which overwrote the good
      manifest as if it were complete: the records in the refused chunk stopped mapping to
      their local files, so `push` ignored edits to them and `repair --prune` deleted them.
      That is the exact data loss REV-133 closed, one step later. A partial read is now
      reported as skipped too, and the carry-forward **merges** (fresh records win) instead
      of replacing, so only what the refused part would have dropped is restored. Covered by
      four tests in `manifestBuilderResilience.test.ts`, each verified to fail without the
      fix.

Notes on this pass: REV-170/REV-171 (missing axios timeout on two clients),
REV-148/REV-192 (fail-open guardrail coercion at two nesting levels), REV-147/REV-190
(whole-value over-redaction in two patterns) and REV-132/REV-139 (two blind spots in one
self-test script) are each one underlying defect fixed at two sites. REV-149 → REV-150 →
REV-151 form a single `dryRun`-integrity programme. Two tags carry a headline claim
broader than the code and disclose the gap in the same comment block (REV-146's unlocked
fallthrough, REV-158's unfenced `assignee`/`reporter`) — both are carried as follow-ups.
Cosmetic: every test name in `mcp-policyRev192.test.js` is prefixed `REV-192:` although
seven of its ten malformed-config cases belong to REV-148.

### Eighth adversarial pass (2026-07-28 … 2026-07-29) — REV-194 … REV-204
An eighth pass, run because the seventh closed two clusters (`dryRun` integrity,
git-argument parsing) whose *neighbours* had never been re-examined under the new
model, and because the seventh's own regression tests had not been checked for
whether they can actually fail. Same three-lens verification panel as the seventh
(`reproduce` / `already-handled` / `consequence`, each lens told to default to
*refuted* under uncertainty, survival on a 2-of-3 majority), so "refuted" again
means *not proven*, not *proven safe*. Every fix below ships a regression test that
was **proven to fail on the pre-fix code** — where the proof needed the fix removed,
the file was restored afterwards and checked byte-identical.
- [x] **REV-194. git's main-option region ran on allow-anything-not-known-dangerous** —
      the region before the subcommand was the last one still on the model REV-124 and
      REV-140 had already replaced everywhere else. REV-117's `GIT_VALUE_OPTIONS` merely
      *skipped* a redirect flag's value so the real subcommand could be found, then let
      the invocation through when that subcommand was read-only. The redirect **is** the
      vector, and read-only verbs are exactly where it lands:
      `git --exec-path=/tmp/evil ls-remote https://host/r` makes git resolve its helper
      binaries from the caller's directory and executes `/tmp/evil/git-remote-https`,
      while `ls-remote` kept the invocation off the confirmation path entirely. `-C`,
      `--git-dir`, `--work-tree`, `--namespace` and `--super-prefix` likewise point git
      at a repository the caller chose. Replaced with a `GIT_SAFE_MAIN_OPTIONS`
      allowlist of flags that provably name no program, no repository and no file —
      everything else confirms. The attached and detached spellings collapse into one
      rule (`--exec-path=/x` and `--exec-path /x` are both simply "not on the
      allowlist"), so no option needs its value skipped any more: the walk returns
      first. Verified against real git 2.50.1. Three previously-allowed cases moved to
      MUST_CONFIRM in the policy fixtures, including `git -C /repo status`.
- [x] **REV-195. four tools honoured `dryRun` and the runtime did not believe them** —
      one defect class at three sites. (a) `sync_generate_scope_knowledge`,
      `sync_generate_scope_docs`, `sync_scope_knowledge_auto_update` and
      `sync_generate_table_dependency_report` declare `dryRun` in their public input
      schema *and* branch on it (`scopeKnowledgeHandlers.ts:252,:407,:458,:507`), but
      were never added to `DRY_RUN_AWARE_TOOLS` — so `isEffectiveDryRun` said false and
      `auditToolCall` stamped `dryRun: false` on a run that wrote nothing. A forensic
      reader saw a real write where a simulation had happened. (b) `sync_set_scope`'s
      validator never typed `dryRun`, so `dryRun: "true"` (the string) passed validation
      and then failed the handler's `=== true` test: a caller asking for a simulation
      silently got the real scope switch. (c) `sync_set_scope`, `sync_set_update_set`
      and `sync_prepare_session` described `dryRun` in their **output** schema ("when
      dryRun is requested, the payload is the dry-run plan") but never declared it as an
      **input**, so a client reading the contract could not discover the only way to
      obtain that payload. `dryRunAwareTools.contract.test.js` now derives the expected
      membership from the tool schemas in **both** directions — declared-but-unregistered
      and registered-but-undeclared — so the hand-maintained list cannot drift again.
- [x] **REV-196. a tool input inverted its meaning without a version bump** —
      `sync_unified_change_workflow`'s `dryRun` property was always declared, but REV-150
      changed what it *means*: the flag never reached the handler, so
      `{apply: true, dryRun: true}` used to perform the real apply, with the preflight
      gate skipped precisely because `index.ts` saw a dry run. `metadata.version` exists
      so clients can cache tool definitions, which means a client would never learn that
      the semantics of an input it already knew about had inverted. Bumped to `1.2.0` and
      the description now states the precedence outright ("takes precedence over
      `apply`: a dry run never mutates the instance"). Recorded because the first draft
      of this entry claimed the *property* was new — `git log -S` disproved that, and the
      rationale was rewritten before the bump was applied.
- [x] **REV-197. two `dryRun` enforcement points trusted the request instead of the
      tool** — (a) `index.ts` skipped the preflight gate whenever the caller merely
      *asked* for a dry run. That is safe only while `MUTATING_TOOLS` stays a subset of
      `DRY_RUN_AWARE_TOOLS`, so the first mutating tool added without a dry-run-aware
      handler would silently lose its preflight — the exact mechanism by which REV-150
      turned `sync_unified_change_workflow`'s dry run into an ungated apply. Now keyed on
      `isEffectiveDryRun`, i.e. a dry run the tool actually honours. (b) `policyConfig`'s
      `requireDryRun` guardrail — an operator lock meaning "this tool may only be
      PLANNED here" — checked the caller's requested flag, so on a tool whose handler
      ignores `dryRun` the lock inverted into its opposite: `dryRun: true` became the
      ONLY accepted shape and it still performed the real mutation, with the audit record
      and the preflight skip both agreeing it was a simulation. An unenforceable lock now
      fails closed: a `requireDryRun` entry naming a tool that does not implement
      `dryRun` is refused outright rather than silently passing.
- [x] **REV-198. `sn_search_scripts` dropped unrecognized table names in silence** —
      `tables` was a free-form string array (no enum) and this tool has no input
      validator, so `tables: ["sys_ui_action"]` — a real script-bearing table that is
      simply not one of the six it covers — searched **nothing** and still returned
      `searchComplete: true, matchCount: 0`. That is the same false clean-search verdict
      REV-152 removed for *failed* queries, arrived at from the other direction, and it
      is the answer that gets acted on ("this identifier is unused, safe to delete").
      Unknown names are now reported in `unknownTables`, `searchComplete` requires
      `errors.length === 0 && unknownTables.length === 0 && tables.length > 0`, and the
      schema enumerates the searchable set so a client can avoid the mistake in the first
      place (spelled out as literals, not imported — `toolSchemas.ts` is deliberately
      dependency-free because the contract gate loads it for its literals alone).
      Metadata `1.1.0`.
- [x] **REV-202. an ADF code block could break out of its own fence** — `renderCodeBlock`
      interpolated **both** the block's content and `attrs.language` raw into a fixed
      three-backtick fence. Both are authored by whoever wrote the Jira issue or a comment
      on it, so content carrying its own ``` line closed the fence early and everything
      after it escaped into the surrounding document; a newline in the info string ended
      the opening-fence line and did the same thing one line higher. This output is read
      by an AI agent (`jira_get_issue`, `syncrona jira`) on a server that also exposes
      code-execution tools, so an escape converts untrusted data into apparent structure —
      an indirect-prompt-injection primitive, not a formatting nit. The fence is now sized
      to `max(3, longestBacktickRun(code) + 1)` per CommonMark, counting **every** run
      rather than only whole-line ones, and the info string must match a conservative
      allow-list (`/^[A-Za-z0-9+#._-]{1,32}$/`, which keeps `c++`, `c#`, `objective-c`,
      `asp.net`, `shell_session`) or is dropped — a mangled language tag is worthless
      while a dropped one costs nothing but highlighting. Scope was narrowed by auditing
      every sibling renderer rather than reflexively escaping: the fence is the only
      construct that can be broken out of, because this is a plain-text renderer — inline
      marks are dropped, headings and blockquotes emit text with no `#`/`>` prefix, links
      emit a bare URL, and table cells joined with ` | ` have no wrapper and no separator
      row, so the output was never a Markdown table and escaping pipes would mangle prose
      for no security gain. That conclusion is recorded in the code so a future reader
      does not redo the audit. `packages/jira/test/adfFence.test.ts` — 11 tests written
      against the *containment property* rather than a fixed fence length, so a future
      change of fencing strategy stays covered; reverting the fix gives 7 failures.
- [x] **REV-203. the remediation text named a credential source the caller had not
      used** — REV-130 made an explicit `--profile` the **exclusive** source
      (`configFromEnv` is never consulted for it), which turned the generic "set
      JIRA_BASE_URL and JIRA_TOKEN" advice into instructions that provably cannot fix the
      call: the user sets the variables, retries, gets the identical error, and has no way
      to discover that the profile path ignores them by design. Three surfaces, three
      shapes of the same defect: `syncrona jira --profile prod` with nothing stored; the
      401 hint, which fires *after* a config was resolved and so must name whichever
      source produced it; and `jira-login`'s own failure hint, which told the user to
      re-run `jira-login` and check environment variables for credentials they had typed
      at the prompt seconds earlier. The messages moved into `packages/jira/src/messages.ts`
      as functions of the credential source (`noJiraConfigMessage(explicitProfile?)`,
      `jiraAuthRecheckHint(explicitProfile?)`) and the MCP handler was fixed alongside the
      CLI, where it matters more: an agent reads the text and acts on it literally. The
      core suite's module mock now imports the **real** helpers — a hand-rolled stub would
      return profile-correct text no matter what the SUT passed in, which is vacuous for a
      defect whose essence is a message that ignored the path taken.
- [x] **REV-204. the guard that pins the CI chain matched comment prose** — REV-133 added
      `ciCheckChain.test.js` to re-derive the root `check` chain and assert every link is
      executed by the workflow, but `runsDirectly()` regexed the whole file **as text**.
      `ci.yml` documents its hand-replicated chain in prose, so `npm run build` and
      `npm run check` appear in comments a few lines above the steps that run them —
      commenting out `run: npm run verify:pack` left the suite fully green, and
      `runsDirectly('check')` returned true for a script that is not a CI step at all.
      The guard stopped guarding at exactly the moment it was needed. It now collects the
      value of every `run:` key — inline scalars plus `run: |` block bodies, skipping any
      line whose first non-blank character is `#` (a YAML comment at step level, a shell
      comment inside a block: not executed either way) — and matches only against that.
      Honestly documented as a line scanner, not a YAML lexer, with a tripwire test that
      fails loudly if `ci.yml` is ever rewritten into a shape the scanner cannot read,
      rather than letting every assertion pass vacuously. Two fixture tests pin the
      commented-out-step and block-scalar cases directly; the real workflow was
      temporarily broken to prove the guard fails (2 failures), then restored and verified
      byte-identical by SHA-256.
- [x] **REV-199. REFUTED — the typescript-plugin's documented TS6 behaviour is the
      behaviour it has.** Raised by the docs-drift lens as "the CHANGELOG describes an
      ES2021 default, a `Bundler` resolution and a suppressed `"use strict"` prologue
      that the code does not implement". Reading `packages/typescript-plugin/src/index.ts`
      in full disproved every clause: the ES2021 default is at `:92-94`, the
      `Bundler`-only-where-TypeScript-would-pick-`Classic` narrowing at `:113-122`, and
      the prologue suppression — scoped to a configuration that set neither `strict` nor
      `alwaysStrict` — at `:97-99` and `:146-148`. Commit `9b41d38` ("chore(deps): migrate
      breaking majors and modernize the toolchain") is the closer, and the behaviour was
      already pinned by two tests (`test/index.test.js:36-39` asserts the exact emit with
      no prologue; `:42-54` asserts `**` survives by default and downlevels only under an
      explicit ES2015). The finding was stale — inherited from a pass that predated the
      migration. No code change. What it *did* surface is REV-200, immediately below.
- [x] **REV-200. the typescript-plugin README documented its configuration precedence
      backwards** — the "Order of Configurations" section listed `sync.config.js` options
      first and then said `tsconfig.json` "override[s] any overlapping values". The merge
      runs the other way round (`src/index.ts:68-83`): the parsed tsconfig is the base and
      plugin `compilerOptions` are `Object.assign`-ed on top, so the plugin option wins.
      A user following the README would set `target` in plugin options *expecting the
      project tsconfig to win*, get the opposite emit, and have nothing tell them. Neither
      the behaviour nor the prose was pinned by anything, so the two could disagree
      indefinitely — no quality gate reads this file (verified before editing: only the
      package's own test parses it, for the ```javascript example block). Confirmed
      empirically in both directions against the built `dist/` before writing a word: a
      tsconfig at ES2015 plus a plugin option at ES2021 keeps `2 ** 3`, while the mirror
      image emits `Math.pow(2, 3)`. Rewrote the section as the real three-level order
      (tsconfig → plugin options → plugin defaults) and documented each default with the
      reason it exists. Four tests added: the two precedence directions (the mirror case
      is what stops the first from passing merely because ES2021 is also the default), a
      docs test that asserts the README lists the base before the override, and one
      pinning that a *configured* `strict` still emits the prologue — without it,
      "suppress the prologue" could regress into "never emit one" unnoticed. All four
      proven to fail on reverted code (README reverted → 1 failure; the merge order
      inverted → 2 failures; `strictnessIsImplied` forced true → 1 failure), with
      `src/index.ts` and `README.md` each restored and verified byte-identical by SHA-256.
      Suite: 23 pass / 0 fail.
- [x] **REV-201. the one place a typo'd `SN_AUTH_METHOD` still resolved silently was the
      code that transmits the credential** — the seventh pass made `resolveAuthMethod`
      report an unrecognized selector as an issue, which fixed `syncrona doctor` and the
      MCP server's startup gate. Two holes survived, both found by asking which callers
      actually *read* the issues list. (a) `servicenowCore.ts` excused the whole list
      under mutual TLS (`issues.length > 0 && !hasMtls`), which re-opened the exact hole
      the selector check was added to close: `SN_AUTH_METHOD=oauth-jwt` (the real alias is
      `jwt-bearer`) plus a client certificate plus leftover client id/secret/password
      resolved to `oauth-password` and posted the user's password to `/oauth_token.do`,
      with the server starting clean — while README:183-190 promises it "refuses to
      start". A client certificate supplies transport identity, so it can legitimately
      stand in for missing Authorization *material*; it says nothing about which grant
      runs. The gate now fails closed on the selector regardless of mTLS
      (`!hasMtls || resolved.unknownExplicit`) and says so, naming the method it fell back
      to. (b) `snClient.ts` computed the issues at both resolve paths and dropped them, so
      every CLI command that authenticates stayed silent while `doctor` complained.
      Deliberately a **warning, not a throw**: `doctor` and `status` resolve credentials to
      run their own checks, so hard-failing there would delete the diagnostic that explains
      the problem. Deliberately scoped to the **selector only**: `init`, `login` and
      `check-env` all resolve credentials before any exist, so warning on every issue would
      print "basic requires SN_PASSWORD." during ordinary first-run setup — a missing field
      announces itself at the first request, an unrecognized selector never does. That
      needed the message as a first-class field rather than `issues[0]`, so
      `ResolvedAuthMethod` gained `unknownExplicitIssue`. Deduped through a module-level
      `Set` (eight call sites, no memoization) with an exported `resetAuthIssueWarnings()`
      test seam. Three tests, each proven to fail on the pre-fix code: mTLS + a typo'd
      selector must throw and must not echo a credential value (gate narrowed back to
      `!hasMtls` → 1 failure); the typo must warn exactly once naming the rejected
      selector (warn removed → 2 failures); and an incomplete-but-untypo'd config must
      warn **not at all** — that last one is what pins the noise decision, and it is the
      assertion that fires when the warn is widened to the full issues list (proven: 1
      failure). Both source files restored byte-identical by SHA-256 after each proof.

### Ninth adversarial pass (2026-07-29 … 2026-07-30) — REV-205 … REV-212 + verification axes
The eight passes before this one all worked the same way: read the code adversarially,
argue about what a finding implies, fix it, add a regression test. That loop has a
blind spot it cannot see past — it can only find what a reader thinks to look for, and
it takes the *tests* on faith. So the ninth pass spent most of its effort on the
verification apparatus itself, and then let the improved apparatus find the code
defects. It worked: every unmarked finding in the second group below was surfaced by a
property test or by a mutation survivor, not by a human reading a diff. Two axes the
repository simply did not have are now measured (per-file coverage floors, mutation
score), and two gates that existed but were never wired into a chain now run in both
`npm run check` and CI.

#### Fixes found by reading (REV-205 … REV-212)
- [x] **REV-205. releasing the collaboration lock deleted whatever file sat at the
      path** — `releaseCollaborationLock` unlinked the lock unconditionally, and a lock
      legitimately changes hands *while its original owner is still running*: a push
      that outlives the 30-minute age window is judged stale by a collaborator, who
      atomically reclaims the path. When the long push then reached its `finally`, it
      deleted the collaborator's **live** lock and the next push walked straight in —
      two pushes writing the same records with no mutual exclusion, which is the one
      thing this lock exists to prevent. A pid cannot establish ownership either (the
      same pid can hold the lock twice in a row, and a lock written on another host may
      carry a pid that matches ours), so the lock payload gained an `owner` token minted
      per acquisition (`randomUUID`), remembered in a module-level `heldLockOwner`, and
      compared before the unlink. Refusing to delete is the safe direction: a lock we
      cannot prove is ours is left in place, and once this process exits its pid stops
      answering `process.kill(pid, 0)`, so the staleness path reclaims it immediately —
      nobody waits out the age window for our restraint. `owner` is optional so a legacy
      lock still parses; such a lock is never recognized as ours, which fails safe.
      A repeated release is now harmless too, which it was not before: the second call
      used to delete whichever lock the *next* push had meanwhile acquired.
- [x] **REV-208. a JWT private key was reproduced verbatim in a filesystem error
      message** — `readPemMaterial` (`snClient.ts`, and the same function in the MCP
      server's `servicenowCore.ts`) treats any value without PEM armor as a path and
      calls `readFileSync` on it. Node puts the path argument into the message verbatim
      (`ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '<value>'`, or `ENAMETOOLONG` past the
      OS filename limit) and also hangs it off `err.path` — so the standard way to carry
      a key in one environment variable or CI secret, base64 with no armor, is a private
      key printed in full to stderr, into the audit trail, and into the error text
      returned to an MCP client. The audit secret scanner cannot save it: that scanner
      matches literal `-----BEGIN … PRIVATE KEY-----` armor, which is precisely the
      shape this function short-circuits on, so a non-armored key structurally never
      matches. The replacement message keeps every fact a genuine path typo needs — which
      setting was read, that it was read as a path, the fs error code, and the value's
      byte length — and carries none of the value. The original error is deliberately
      **not** attached as `cause`: enough loggers stringify a cause chain that keeping it
      would re-open the same leak one indirection later.
- [x] **REV-209. the build-tree writer never got INJ-1's containment guard** — INJ-1
      hardened the *source*-tree writer (`writeSNFileCurry`) precisely because a
      manifest-supplied filename is untrusted there. The build tree was left on the old
      model: `getBuildExt` returns the manifest's `type` verbatim, `pushPipeline`
      interpolates it (`${relPathNoExt}.${buildExt}`), joins it onto the build root, and
      `writeFileForce` is a bare `fsp.writeFile` with no containment check of any kind —
      so a manifest `type` of `js/../../../../evil` escapes the build directory *and* the
      workspace. Half a threat model enforced is not a threat model. Validated at
      `getBuildExt` rather than at the write site, because that is the single point where
      the untrusted value leaves the manifest, so every build layout and any future
      caller is covered by one check — and with `isSafePathComponent`, the same rule the
      download side uses, so the two cannot drift.
- [x] **REV-210. the process-kill escalation had no budget and two ways to settle
      twice** — after a timeout fired, `processRunner` sent SIGTERM and then waited on a
      child that may never exit; the settle promise could also resolve from more than one
      path. Rewritten around a single exit from the promise plus an explicit escalation
      budget (SIGTERM immediately, SIGKILL after the budget), so a child that ignores
      SIGTERM cannot hold a tool call open indefinitely and a settled call cannot settle
      again.
- [x] **REV-211. `sanitizeForAudit` walked attacker-influenced data unbounded on both
      axes** — `args` is whatever an MCP client sends, and the sanitizer recursed through
      it to build the audit line. Depth alone is not enough (a wide object at shallow
      depth is just as expensive as a deep one), so the walk is now bounded on depth
      *and* breadth, with the truncation stated in the record rather than silently
      applied — an audit line that quietly dropped fields would be worse than one that
      says it was truncated.
- [x] **REV-212. the ATF poll's `timeoutMs` was spent per request, not per poll** —
      `handleRunAtfTests` treats `timeoutMs` as the budget for the whole poll, and the
      docs say so, but the implementation applied it to each individual request; a poll
      of N requests could therefore run for N × the budget the caller set. Now the
      budget is checked against elapsed wall time for the poll as a whole. The regression
      suite mocks the clock instead of sleeping, so it asserts the arithmetic rather than
      racing it.

#### Fixes the new verification apparatus found
Each of these came out of a property test or a mutation survivor, and each is a defect
eight adversarial reading passes had walked past.
- [x] **a reference field flattened to an object aborted the download of every table** —
      `TableAPIRecord` is typed `Record<string, string>`, but that is an unvalidated
      claim about a remote response, and it is wrong in a routine rather than adversarial
      way: ServiceNow flattens a reference column to its bare value only when
      `sysparm_exclude_reference_link=true`, which `snClient.tableAPIGet` never sets, so
      every reference column arrives as `{ link, value }`. A `tableOptions.displayField`
      or `differentiatorField` pointing at a reference column therefore handed an
      **object** to `String.prototype.replace`, and the resulting TypeError escaped
      `buildBulkDownloadFromTableAPI`'s per-table catch (it is not a skippable HTTP
      error) — aborting the download of *every* table with `name.replace is not a
      function` and naming neither the table nor the record. A property test shrank it to
      one requested record and one returned row `{}`. Introduced `fieldText`, which takes
      `.value` for the reference shape precisely so a name derived here is identical to
      one derived from a response that did set the parameter.
- [x] **a record whose `sys_id` was `".."` materialized the parent directory** — the
      all-dots guard added earlier rejected `.`/`..` in the *display* value and then
      returned `record.sys_id` unchecked, so a row whose sys_id was itself `..` walked
      straight through the check that had just fired. A property test shrank it to
      `{ sys_id: ".." }`. Both the name and the sys_id are now checked as path
      components — the sys_id too, because it is interpolated into the directory name
      whenever names collide (`${name}_${sysId}`), so an unusable sys_id escapes through
      the name. A row that cannot be named or keyed is dropped with a counted warning
      instead of becoming an `undefined` folder that no push can ever target.
- [x] **a record named `__proto__` vanished silently** — `records[name] = record` on an
      object literal invokes the inherited setter for that one key instead of creating a
      property, so the record disappeared: `Object.keys` did not list it, the manifest
      never mentioned it, the downloader never wrote it, and the run reported success. If
      it was the table's only record the whole table disappeared from the result.
      `__proto__` is a legal ServiceNow display name, a legal directory name, and also
      arrives as a *supplied* manifest name (`JSON.parse` makes it an own property).
      Stored via `Object.defineProperty` as the own, enumerable, JSON-serializable
      property every consumer already assumes it is.
- [x] **three prototype-chain lookups returned functions typed as strings** — the same
      bug class in three modules, all keyed by a name that comes from outside the
      process. `getDisplayField("constructor")` returned the `Object` *function* typed
      `string` and truthy enough to pass the `|| "name"` default, from where it is sent
      as a `sysparm_fields` entry; `SN_TYPE_MAP[internal_type]` did the same for a value
      that is interpolated into a filename; and `toolArgSchemas[toolName]` returned a
      truthy non-schema for a client calling the tool `"constructor"` — `index.ts` hands
      `request.params.name` to the validator *before* any is-this-a-real-tool check, so
      `schema.safeParse is not a function` surfaced as an internal error instead of a
      clean `INVALID_ARGUMENTS`. All three now go through an own-property check, matching
      the `normalizeAuthMethod` guard that had the same hole.
- [x] **the identifier gate declared values clean and then passed the unclean original
      through** — every top-level identifier schema `.trim()`s before matching its regex,
      so parsing `" <sys_id> "` succeeds; keeping only `parsed.success` and discarding
      `parsed.data` reported "valid" while leaving the untrimmed string in the arguments
      the handler goes on to use. That happened for any field the tool's own schema had
      not already normalized — an extra key on a `looseObject` (`sn_query_records` +
      `sysId`), or any tool with no `toolArgSchemas` entry at all (`sn_search_scripts` +
      `table`) — and the characters that escaped are exactly the ones `trim` strips,
      newlines included, so a value the gate had called regex-clean reached the Table API
      URL builder and the audit line. The normalized value is now written back, and a
      whitespace-only identifier is normalized to `""` rather than passed through:
      "treat as not supplied" only holds downstream if the value is falsy there, and
      handlers test `args.table`/`args.sysId` for truthiness, so `" "` arrived as a
      supplied-but-blank table name.
- [x] **`repair --apply --prune` deleted files the manifest does claim** — the orphan
      predicate compared record names byte-exactly while `SNFileExists` decides "already
      present" with `fsp.stat`, which is case-insensitive on APFS and NTFS, the defaults
      on macOS and Windows. A manifest record `Foo` whose folder is on disk as `foo` was
      therefore never reported missing (stat found it) and never claimed by the orphan
      lookup either — so prune deleted it, along with any local edits not yet pushed.
      `canonicalName` now case-folds, which is what `manifestBuilder` already does when
      it decides two record names collide, so the two modules agree on when two names are
      "the same name". `toLowerCase`, never `toLocaleLowerCase`: the latter is
      locale-dependent (Turkish dotless ı) and would make pruning depend on the
      operator's locale. Merging two records that differ only in case can only ever make
      this predicate refuse to delete.
- [x] **the missing-record probe looked in a directory the writer never uses** — it
      probed `<table>/<manifest key>` while the writer writes `<table>/<rec.name>`. The
      two agree in a manifest this CLI produced, but nothing enforces it for a
      hand-edited or foreign one, and where they diverged the record was permanently
      stuck: the probe found nothing, every run re-downloaded it, the writer put it back
      under `rec.name`, and `repair --prune` then deleted that file as an orphan. The
      probe now follows the writer, falling back to the key so a record whose `name` a
      manifest omitted stays probeable.
- [x] **`isDestructiveWorkspaceCommand` decided the gate on the first operand** —
      resolving "the" CLI subcommand means knowing which tokens are option *values*, and
      that is a per-option question: `--logLevel debug push`, `--instance-profile dev
      push` and `-d main push` all put a value where the first operand would be, and all
      three really run push (verified against the CLI's yargs registration). So the gate
      reported `requiresConfirmation === false` for a live push to a ServiceNow instance.
      An enumeration of the value-taking options would re-open every time the CLI grows
      one, so the check is default-deny in the same way the git option regions are: any
      destructive verb **anywhere** in the operand region confirms. `findSyncroCliSubcommand`
      survives for display purposes with its best-effort limitation documented, and its
      doc comment now points security decisions at the other function.

#### Verification axis 1 — per-file coverage floors (GATE-2, GATE-3)
Both packages had a global coverage ratchet and nothing else, and a global-only ratchet
is a gate one weak file hides behind. Measured, not assumed: `dist/scopeBootstrap.js`
really did sit at 24.32% under a green gate.
- [x] Core: the two tree-wide `coverageThreshold` globs moved from 20/20 — a floor only
      against a file with no test at all — to 62 branches / 78 lines, a few points under
      the weakest legitimate file in the tree (`mcpCommand.ts`, 83.33% lines / 68.18%
      branches, whose uncovered code spawns the MCP server and branches on
      `process.platform === "win32"`). On top of that, **21 per-module floors** on
      everything that writes files, pushes to the instance, holds credentials, resolves a
      scope or shells out — each pinned ~3pts under its measured lines and ~4-5pts under
      its measured branches, with the measurement recorded inline as `// measured L/B`.
      The asymmetry is deliberate: branch coverage is the metric that moves between macOS
      and Linux CI (keychain, homedir, platform). Verified against `@jest/reporters` that
      the mechanics hold: a covered file is pushed into *every* threshold group it
      matches, so adding keys cannot disable the globs or the global ratchet; a key that
      matches nothing fails loudly ("Coverage data for <key> was not found"), so a floor
      orphaned by a rename is reported rather than silently gating nothing; and keys
      resolve against `process.cwd()`, not `rootDir`, which is why the suite must run
      from the package.
- [x] MCP server: `PER_FILE_LINE_FLOOR` 10 → 80 (weakest of the 55 reported modules is
      88.17%), a new `PER_FILE_BRANCH_FLOOR` of 45, and **19 module floors** for policy
      evaluation, input validation, the audit trail, the tool wrappers, transport/scope
      handling and anything that starts a process or a listener. The branch floor is the
      one that matters: deleting the guard case of an `if` keeps every line executed by
      the happy path, so a line-only floor cannot see a safety check losing its only
      negative test. A floor whose pattern matches nothing in the report **fails** the
      gate (`findStaleFloors`) — that is how a per-file gate quietly stops gating.
- [x] GATE-3: a gate nobody has seen fail is a hypothesis, so `coverageFloors.test.ts`
      (core) and `coverageGatePerFile.test.js` (MCP) drive the threshold logic directly
      and prove each floor rejects a file below it, that the per-file floor stays under
      the aggregate ratchet, and that a stale floor is reported.

#### Verification axis 2 — gate parity (local `check` ≡ CI)
- [x] `bench:guard` and `scan:secrets` were in neither `npm run check` nor CI. Both are
      now links in the chain and steps in the workflow. `scripts/bench.mjs` had supported
      `--max-ms` since G14 and nothing ever passed a threshold, so the "baseline" was a
      number a human compared by hand; the guard runs the same CPU-bound manifest/doc
      path with a deliberately loose ceiling (25 ms against a 4.9 ms median measured
      while the machine was *under load*, because a shared CI runner is the loaded case
      and a performance gate that flakes gets disabled). It is a blowup detector for an
      accidental O(n²), not a microbenchmark.
- [x] `scripts/scan-secrets.mjs` is the developer-side mirror of the CI `secret-scan`
      job, running gitleaks over the working tree with the same `.gitleaks.toml`. When
      gitleaks is absent it prints an explicit SKIPPED notice rather than a false pass —
      the hosted runners are exactly that case, and the enforcing gate remains the CI job
      that scans the full history from a pinned action. This mattered concretely: the
      scan used to live only in CI, so `npm run check` could be exit 0 on a tree CI would
      reject, which is how a fixture value reached the default branch and turned it red.
- [x] `ciCheckChain.test.js` re-derives the chain from the root `package.json` and fails
      the build when a link has no matching step in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, so the
      two gates cannot drift apart again. `npm run test:mutation` is deliberately **not**
      a link — a mutation run is far too slow for a per-change gate.
- [x] `.stryker-tmp/` and `**/reports/mutation/` are now git-ignored. Stryker copies the
      entire package root into its sandbox, including `.syncrona-mcp/` runtime state and
      the git-excluded AI-harness files; those exclusions are path-anchored and do not
      cover a nested copy, so an unignored sandbox turns local-only files into
      committable ones. It is also expensive — three leftover sandboxes grew the tree the
      local secret scan walks from 37 MB / 2 s to 108 MB / 13 s.

#### Verification axis 3 — property-based tests (3 files → 8)
Added `manifestBuilder.property.test.ts` (core), `auth.property.test.ts` (sn-transport),
`inputValidation.property.test.js` and `safetyPolicy.property.test.js` (MCP), and
extended `adf.property.test.ts` (jira). These are what found the reference-object crash,
the `sys_id: ".."` escape and the `__proto__` disappearance above — three defects that
example-based tests had no reason to reach, each shrunk by the runner to a minimal
witness small enough to paste into a regression test.

#### Verification axis 4 — measured mutation score (W1/W2)
Stryker 8.7.1, `testRunner: "command"` over the compiled `dist/` (`npx tsc && node
--test test/*.test.js`), run from the repo root because ~26 governance tests read root
artifacts through `../../`. First real numbers for this repository:

| module | score | killed | timeout | survived |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `endpointPolicy.ts` | **100.00%** | 24 | 0 | 0 |
| `inputValidation.ts` | **86.99%** | 107 | 0 | 16 |
| `createTablePolicy.ts` | **83.33%** | 60 | 0 | 12 |
| `safetyPolicy.ts` | **63.14%** → **94.90%** | 607 → 985 | 49 → 1 | 383 → 53 |

- [x] The `safetyPolicy.ts` number is the finding. **327 of its 383 survivors are
      `StringLiteral` mutants**, and ~85% of those are one shape: empty a single entry
      out of a policy-name `Set`. The suite tested the *helpers* thoroughly and the
      *table contents* barely at all — so deleting `"pkill"` from `BLOCKED_COMMANDS`, or
      `"ls-remote"` from the 27 read-only git verbs, changed no test result. On a
      denylist that is a security hole; on an allowlist it is over-confirmation.
- [x] The response: 34 targeted kill-proofs, plus
      `safetyPolicy.tableCompleteness.test.js` — a behavioural contract suite that
      asserts the observable policy decision through the public API for *every entry of
      every table*: 8 blocked commands, 4 shell interpreters, 8 shell tokens, 3
      destructive CLI subcommands, 3 package runners, 18 dry-run-aware tools, the 6-entry
      read-only allowlist, 13 git main options, 27 read-only git verbs, **302** safe
      subcommand options, and 41 safe short flags plus the 11 held-out uppercase ones
      that must confirm. Suite total: 1585 → **1610** MCP tests.
- [x] **Design rule, learned the hard way and recorded so it is not re-learned:** a
      table-completeness fixture must be a **literal list inside the test file**. A list
      derived by parsing `src/safetyPolicy.ts` at runtime moves *with* the mutant, so the
      assertion stays true and the mutant survives — the test then measures nothing while
      looking rigorous.
- [x] `servicenowCore.ts` and `policyConfig.ts` are deliberately out of mutation scope:
      ~32 s per mutant × 1639 lines is over two hours for one module. Recorded as a
      scoping decision, not an oversight.
- [x] **Re-measured (2026-07-31), and the fix holds.** `safetyPolicy.ts` alone, 1039
      mutants: **94.90%** — 985 killed, 1 timeout, 53 survived, 0 no-coverage. The
      `StringLiteral` class that was the whole finding collapsed from **327 survivors to
      9**; what is left is no longer a table-coverage gap but a scattered tail of 18
      `ConditionalExpression`, 7 `BlockStatement` and 5 `MethodExpression` mutants across
      the normalisation helpers (`safetyPolicy.ts:123`, `:144-152`, `:194-214`). Caveat
      kept honest: Stryker scores that 1 timeout as a kill, so the true figure is
      marginally below 94.90%. Triage with
      `node --max-old-space-size=8192 scripts/mutation-triage.mjs reports/mutation/<name>.json`
      — the JSON report for a single module of this size is ~350 MB, which is why the
      default heap is not enough.
- [x] **`packages/core` measured for the first time (2026-07-31).** The core config had
      never actually been run — the number below is the baseline, not a regression check.
      750 mutants over the five modules in scope, **14 min 38 s** with the jest runner and
      `coverageAnalysis: "perTest"` (14.21 tests per mutant, so the per-test narrowing is
      doing real work — the command runner in mcp-server has no such option and pays the
      whole suite per mutant).

      | module | score | killed | timeout | survived | no cov |
      |---|---|---|---|---|---|
      | `genericUtils.ts` | **97.17%** | 51 | 52 | 2 | 1 |
      | `FileUtils.ts` | **91.11%** | 138 | 108 | 18 | 6 |
      | `downloadCheckpoint.ts` | **81.82%** | 22 | 5 | 6 | 0 |
      | `repairCommand.ts` | **80.84%** | 197 | 14 | 44 | 6 |
      | `PluginManager.ts` | **78.21%** | 41 | 20 | 17 | 0 |
      | **all files** | **86.63%** | 449 | 199 | 87 | 13 |

- [x] **Read that 86.63% as a ceiling, not a figure.** **199 of the 750 mutants (26.5%)
      are timeouts, and Stryker scores a timeout as a kill.** The honest reading is a
      band: if every timeout were instead a survivor the score would be **60.0%**, so the
      true number is somewhere in `[60.0%, 86.63%]` and closer to the top only to the
      extent that those timeouts are genuine infinite loops rather than a loaded machine.
      `FileUtils.ts` (138 killed vs **108** timeouts) and `genericUtils.ts` (51 vs **52**)
      are where that uncertainty lives — both are loop-heavy, so a mutated loop condition
      really does hang, but the run shared the box with other work. **Open:** re-run
      `packages/core` alone on an idle machine before quoting a single number.
- [x] **That re-run happened (2026-08-17) and the machine was never the cause.**
      `packages/core` alone, idle box, default budget: 763 mutants in **14 min 9 s** —
      the same wall clock as the "loaded" baseline's 14 min 38 s — scoring **85.92%**
      with **203 timeouts (26.6%)**, against 26.5% before. The band is therefore
      `[59.2%, 85.92%]` (450 killed of 760 scored, errors excluded) and it is a real
      property of this suite, not an artefact of who else was using the CPU.

      | module | score | killed | timeout | survived | no cov | error |
      |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
      | `genericUtils.ts` | **95.24%** | 37 | 63 | 4 | 1 | 3 |
      | `FileUtils.ts` | **88.34%** | 159 | 91 | 27 | 6 | 0 |
      | `downloadCheckpoint.ts` | **84.85%** | 22 | 6 | 5 | 0 | 0 |
      | `repairCommand.ts` | **81.23%** | 196 | 16 | 43 | 6 | 0 |
      | `PluginManager.ts` | **80.77%** | 36 | 27 | 15 | 0 | 0 |
      | **all files** | **85.92%** | 450 | 203 | 94 | 13 | 3 |

      Getting to that number first required fixing a regression that had switched the
      whole mutation run off — see the `<rootDir>` anchoring of
      `testPathIgnorePatterns` in the 2026-08-17 WORKLOG entry.
- [x] **The budget is not the lever, and the earlier reasoning was corrected.** Raising
      it to `timeoutMS` 30000 / `timeoutFactor` 3 — **2.7x** the effective allowance, not
      6x, since Stryker computes `timeoutFactor * netTime + timeoutMS + timeOverheadMS`
      ([`mutant-test-planner.js:106`](node_modules/@stryker-mutator/core/dist/src/mutants/mutant-test-planner.js))
      — moved the rate from 26.5% to 26.7%. Reverted to the defaults. Two claims written
      down during that pass were wrong and have been removed rather than left standing:
      that the raised budget made the run "about 6x slower" (it did not — 14 m 9 s vs
      14 m 38 s), and that `StringLiteral`/`ArrayDeclaration` mutants "cannot loop"
      (`[]` → `["Stryker was here"]` into a drain-and-refill loop hangs perfectly well).
- [x] **Answered: the timeouts are NOT hangs, and they are NOT stable.** The question
      decides whether the band's top or bottom is honest, so it was measured rather than
      argued. `genericUtils.ts` (108 mutants) was re-run with `coverageAnalysis: "off"`,
      which runs **363 tests per mutant instead of 18** — twenty times the work against
      only 1.65x the budget. A wall-clock explanation predicts far more timeouts; a
      genuine-hang explanation predicts the *same mutants* timing out. Neither happened.
      The rate barely moved (58.3% → 55.6%) but the *identity* of the timeouts churned
      almost completely:

      | perTest → off | count |
      |---|---|
      | Timeout → Timeout | 32 |
      | **Timeout → Killed** | **27** |
      | **Killed → Timeout** | **22** |
      | Killed → Killed | 15 |
      | Survived → Timeout | 2 |
      | **Timeout → Survived** | **1** |
      | other (Survived/RuntimeError/NoCoverage) | 9 |

      An infinite loop times out in both configurations, every time. Only 32 of 60 did.
      **27 mutants scored as "detected by timeout" were provably killed by a real
      assertion**, and — the line that matters — **1 mutant scored as detected was
      provably a SURVIVOR**, with 2 more known survivors scored as kills the other way.
      So a timeout here is a coin flip, and Stryker's "TimedOut counts as DETECTED"
      is silently converting an unknown number of survivors into kills. Corroborating:
      **0 of the 203** timeouts tripped Stryker's instrumented hit limit, which a loop
      through the mutated statement should trip, and the only 3 errors in the run are
      all `genericUtils.ts`, two of them `Test runner crashed. Tried twice to restart
      it without any luck` — a runner fault, not a mutant one.
- [ ] **Open: pin the mechanism, then decide the remedy.** The leading explanation is
      that Stryker calibrates every mutant's budget on a dry run it performs **solo**
      (`Ran 880 tests in 17 seconds (net 7683 ms, overhead 9455 ms)` — `timeOverheadMS`
      is that solo overhead) and then executes mutants **9 at a time** on a 10-core box,
      so the allowance never accounts for the contention the mutants actually run under.
      That also explains the per-module gradient, because `jest.enableFindRelatedTests`
      defaults to on: `genericUtils.ts` is imported almost everywhere, so each of its
      mutants reloads most of the suite (58% timeouts), while `repairCommand.ts` is
      reachable from two test files (6%). Being measured now with `--concurrency 1`.
      Until it is settled, **quote the band `[59.2%, 85.92%]`, never the 85.92%.**
- [x] **The shape of the 87 survivors** is 28 `ConditionalExpression`, 24 `StringLiteral`,
      14 `BlockStatement`, 9 `EqualityOperator` — no single dominant class like the
      `safetyPolicy.ts` finding, so this is a diffuse tail rather than one missing kind of
      test. The weakest real module is `PluginManager.ts` (78.21%, 17 survivors and **0**
      no-coverage — every mutant was executed and 17 still went undetected, which is a
      test-strength gap, not a reachability gap).
- [x] **Hazard, recorded — and its first diagnosis corrected.** A phantom suite failure
      during this work was initially attributed to Stryker rebuilding the shared
      `packages/mcp-server/dist/`. That is **wrong, and verified wrong**: the sandbox
      contains its own real `dist/` directory (not a symlink), rebuilt per mutant at
      14:19 while the checkout's `dist/safetyPolicy.js` still carried its 14:04
      timestamp. The actual hazard is **hand-injecting a mutant into the real `src/`**
      to check whether a test kills it — the REV-201 kill-proof pattern — because any
      concurrent suite run then compiles and executes that mutant and reports failures
      that are not real. Do kill-proofs in a scratch clone, or restore and re-verify by
      checksum before anything else runs. What a live mutation run *does* cost is the
      whole machine: at concurrency 12 with `coverageAnalysis: off`, a heavy suite
      started alongside it can push a sandbox past `timeoutMS`, and Stryker scores a
      timeout as a **kill** — so the score comes out inflated rather than wrong-looking.

#### Verification axis 5 — multi-process race harness for the collaboration lock (REV-233)
`npm run race:lock` (`scripts/lock-race.mjs` + `scripts/lock-race-child.mjs`) spawns N real
processes against one real temp directory, releases them from a busy-wait barrier into the
same millisecond, and imports the compiled `packages/core/dist/pushCommand.js` so the code
under test is the shipped code.

- [x] **Why a process harness and not a unit test.** The lock's two load-bearing mechanisms
      are kernel-level — `link()`'s all-or-nothing publication and `process.kill(pid, 0)`
      liveness. Neither means anything inside one Node process against a mocked filesystem,
      so the unit suites can only assert the logic *around* them. Every real defect this
      lock has had was found here and was invisible to the unit suites.
- [x] **The invariant, after the first version of it was wrong.** "At most one racer per
      round acquires" reported 15/15 failures against correct code: a winner that releases —
      or simply exits, freeing its lock by pid liveness — legitimately hands the lock to a
      racer still inside its retry loop. Sequential handoff is the system working. The two
      sound checks are (1) **temporal overlap** of held intervals, each recorded as a strict
      subset of the true one so any overlap is genuine, and (2) the decisive **self-watch**:
      the holder polls its own lock file synchronously for the whole hold and must never see
      anything but its own pid — no clock reasoning required.
- [x] **What it caught.** Four designs failed here before the current one:

      | design | result |
      |---|---|
      | rename-based reclaim | 2/15 rounds at 3 racers, 9/12 at 5 (three simultaneous winners twice) — the lock path stands empty between rename-aside and restore |
      | mutex-file guard | held at 3 and 5, broke 7/20 at 8 — the mutex is itself a contended path with the same problem one level up |
      | claim + `wx` create | 2/20 at 16 — `O_CREAT\|O_EXCL` publishes the **name** before the **bytes**, so a claim being born reads as empty, and empty reads as abandoned |
      | claim + `wx` on the lock | 1/20 at 16 — the same window on the lock file itself |
      | claim + `link()` | **0 anomalies** (current design) |

- [x] **The fix that came out of it (REV-233).** `writeFile(path, body, {flag:"wx"})` is
      atomic in *exclusion* but not in *publication*. `createExclusiveWithContent()` now
      writes `sync.collaboration.staging.<pid>.<basename>` first and publishes it with
      `fsp.link()`, which has no name/bytes split and still fails `EEXIST` rather than
      overwriting. The post-acquire sweep reaps abandoned staging files and eviction claims.
- [x] **Measured 2026-07-31, 195 rounds over 9 configurations, 0 anomalies:** 20×3 / 20×5 /
      20×8 / 20×12 / 30×16 / 15×24 in `stale` mode, plus 20×12 `fresh`, 20×8 with
      `--release 0`, and 30×12 with `--hold-ms 0`. Contention was real in every row
      (1872 losses, 93 handoffs); zero stolen locks, zero interval overlaps, zero rounds
      with no winner, and no leftover lock, claim or staging file anywhere.
- [x] **The harness refuses to report a false green.** It exits non-zero as `INCONCLUSIVE`
      when a multi-racer run observes no losses *and* no handoffs, because that means the
      racers never met and the run proved nothing. It also rejects non-integer `--rounds` /
      `--racers` / `--hold-ms`: a first matrix run "passed" all 9 configurations instantly
      because zsh does not word-split unquoted expansions, `--rounds` parsed to `NaN`, and
      the round loop executed zero times. A safety check that silently runs zero rounds is
      worse than no safety check — it looks like proof.

#### Verification axis 6 — hostile-input harness over the real MCP stdio boundary
- [x] **The one surface no test touched.** Every other test in `packages/mcp-server`
      calls an exported function directly, so the transport framing, the SDK's JSON-RPC
      parser, a stray `console.log` landing on fd 1 and an unhandled rejection taking the
      process down are all invisible to them — and that boundary is the *only* thing a
      real MCP client sees. `packages/mcp-server/scripts/stdio-fuzz.js` spawns the
      compiled server as a child process, feeds **42** hostile frames down a real pipe
      and checks five invariants after each one:
      1. **STDOUT PURITY** — every line on stdout parses as JSON-RPC. One log line on the
         wrong fd corrupts the stream for the whole session.
      2. **LIVENESS** — a `ping` after each frame must still be answered. No frame may
         wedge or kill the process.
      3. **ID FIDELITY** — every response id was requested, ids are never reused, and a
         notification (no id) is never answered.
      4. **NO LEAKAGE** — error payloads carry no stack frames, no absolute paths and no
         `node_modules`. A model reads these, and a client shows them to a user.
      5. **HERMETICITY** — the corpus must not make the server do real work.
- [x] **Invariant 5 exists because the file's own SCOPE claim was false.** The first draft
      documented the corpus as non-executing while two frames named the real `sync_status`
      tool; dispatch reached the CLI and each spawned a **2–4 s** `syncrona status`
      subprocess. That is what made the run flaky — under load the response missed the
      drain window and the harness reported `unanswered-request: id 1024`. The fix was not
      to widen the timeout. The two frames were retargeted at a name that does not exist
      (the `normalizeTimeout` semantics they were probing are asserted directly in
      `test/toolService.cov.test.js`), and the prose claim became a machine check read from
      the **server's own audit log**: any `tool.call` entry with `ok: true` is a tool that
      ran, and fails the suite. A future frame that reaches real work now breaks the build
      instead of quietly making it slow.
- [x] **Corpus:** truncated and malformed JSON, a lone `\n`, 100 KB of garbage, NUL and
      lone-surrogate bytes, a UTF-8 BOM, CRLF framing, JSON-RPC batches, wrong/missing
      `jsonrpc`, duplicate and colliding ids, `__proto__`/`constructor.prototype` in
      params, `tools/call` with a missing/non-object/array `arguments`, 1000-deep nesting,
      a 1 MB payload, and a bidi-override tool name (Trojan Source, CVE-2021-42574). The
      bidi frame is written as `\u202E`/`\u202C` escapes rather than literal characters:
      as literals the file becomes *binary* to `grep` and `diff` — which silently broke
      several searches while it was being written — and it would itself carry exactly the
      bytes a source scanner exists to flag. Escaped, the file stays ASCII and the same
      bytes still reach the wire.
- [x] **Result: `frames=42 messages=68 stdout=46802B violations=0`**, byte-identical over
      three consecutive runs. Wall time **221 ms** — measured with `execFile` + `Date.now()`
      after both `time` and `date +%s%N` gave untrustworthy figures on macOS/zsh. That cost
      is why it belongs in the default suite rather than behind a flag, and it is not
      incidental: `npm test` globs `test/*.test.js`, which Stryker re-runs **per mutant**,
      so a multi-second test here would multiply across an entire mutation run.
- [x] **`test/stdioFuzz.test.js` guards the harness against a false green** the same way
      the race harness does: it pins the corpus at ≥40 frames with unique names, requires
      seven named frames to still exist, and asserts the run delivered the *whole* corpus —
      the loop breaks on the first wedge, so a violation-free report over three frames
      would otherwise read as success.

#### The MCP handshake reported the wrong version for eight minor releases
- [x] **`SERVER_VERSION` was the literal `"0.1.0"`** in `runtimeConfig.ts` while the package
      was at 0.9.1 — so the `initialize` handshake, `sync_health` and every tool-module
      banner told clients 0.1.0. Found by the stdio harness, which reads the real handshake
      off the wire. Same defect and same fix as `syncrona --version` in 0.9.1: read the
      version from the package's own `package.json` at runtime. The failure fallback is the
      deliberately implausible `"0.0.0-unknown"` rather than a guessed number — if the
      lookup breaks, the handshake should say so instead of asserting something false.
      Verified on the wire: `"serverInfo":{"name":"syncrona-mcp-server","version":"0.9.1"}`.

#### A mutant proved a guard was load-bearing — and left the proof on disk
- [x] **`fileUtilsCoverage.test.ts` asserted on paths it did not own.** Two escape tests
      checked that nothing was written to `path.dirname(root)`, and with `root` from
      `mkdtemp` that resolves to `os.tmpdir()` itself — shared with every process on the
      machine. Not hermetic in either direction: an unrelated `/tmp/escape.js` fails a
      healthy build, and the test cannot clean up after the escape it provokes.
      Both happened. The core mutation run produced a mutant with the traversal guard in
      `FileUtils.ts` removed; the escape then genuinely occurred and wrote `escape.js`
      into `os.tmpdir()`. The mutant was correctly **killed** by the `rejects.toThrow`
      above it, so the run stayed green — but the byte survived, and every later run of
      that file failed on the stale artifact. Fixed with a `makeNestedRoot()` helper that
      puts the source root one level inside a tracked sandbox, so the parent is a directory
      the suite owns and `afterAll` removes. The incident is worth keeping: it is direct
      evidence the traversal guard is load-bearing (without it the write really does
      escape) and a reminder that a test asserting on a shared namespace is a test that
      can be broken by anything.

#### Also added
`cliCommandWiring`, `repairPruneConfirm`, `pemMaterialLeak` (core) and
`pemMaterialLeak`, `processRunnerSettle`, `mcp-auditRev211`, `mcp-atfRev212`,
`scopeBootstrapAutoPull`, `scopePathsCoverage`, `toolServiceGuards` (MCP).

